1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,090
SARAH: Hello. Children's Engineering Workshops.

2
00:00:03,090 --> 00:00:08,890
FATHER: Oh hello. I wanted some information about the workshops in the school holidays.

3
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:09,720
SARAH: Sure.

4
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:12,670
FATHER: I have two daughters who are interested.

5
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:17,680
The younger one's Lydia, she's four - do you take children as young as that?

6
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:24,000
SARAH: Yes, our Tiny Engineers workshop is for four to five-year-olds.

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,270
FATHER: What sorts of activities do they do?

8
00:00:26,270 --> 00:00:31,710
SARAH: All sorts. For example, they work together to design a special cover that goes?

9
00:00:31,710 --> 00:00:34,280
round an egg, so that

10
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:38,600
when it's inside they can drop it from a height and it doesn't break.

11
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,630
Well, sometimes it does break but that's part of the fun!

12
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:46,420
FATHER: Right. And Lydia loves building things.

13
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:50,150
Is there any opportunity for her to do that?

14
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:54,860
SARAH: Well, they have a competition to see who can make the highest tower.

15
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:58,000
You'd be amazed how high they can go.

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:58,470
FATHER: Right.

17
00:00:58,470 --> 00:01:02,540
SARAH: But they're learning all the time as well as having fun.

18
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:06,080
For example, one thing they do is to design

19
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:08,742
and build a car that's attached to a balloon,

20
00:01:08,742 --> 00:01:14,600
and the force of the air in that actually powers the car and makes it move along.

21
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:17,310
They go really fast too.

22
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:20,770
FATHER: OK, well, all this sounds perfect.

23
00:01:20,770 --> 00:01:24,440
FATHER: Now Carly, that's my older daughter,

24
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:31,780
has just had her seventh birthday, so presumably she'd be in a different group?

25
00:01:31,780 --> 00:01:35,420
SARAH: Yes, she'd be in the Junior Engineers.

26
00:01:35,420 --> 00:01:38,480
That's for children from six to eight.

27
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:41,480
FATHER: And do they do the same sorts of activities?

28
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:45,280
SARAH: Some are the same, but a bit more advanced.

29
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:51,068
So they work out how to build model vehicles, things like cars and trucks,

30
00:01:51,068 --> 00:01:56,856
but also how to construct animals using the same sorts of material and technique,

31
00:01:56,856 --> 00:02:02,230
and then they learn how they can program them and make them move.

32
00:02:02,230 --> 00:02:04,430
FATHER: So they learn a bit of coding?

33
00:02:04,430 --> 00:02:07,730
SARAH: They do. They pick it up really quickly.

34
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:12,390
We're there to help if they need it, but they learn from one another too.

35
00:02:12,390 --> 00:02:16,160
FATHER: Right. And do they have competitions too?

36
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:18,722
SARAH: Yes, with the Junior Engineers,

37
00:02:18,722 --> 00:02:24,160
it's to use recycled materials like card and wood to build a bridge

38
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:27,260
and the longest one gets a prize.

39
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:30,600
FATHER: That sounds fun. I wouldn't mind doing that myself!

40
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:33,957
SARAH: Then they have something a bit different,

41
00:02:33,957 --> 00:02:37,260
which is to think up an idea for a five-minute movie

42
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:41,930
and then film it, using special animation software.

43
00:02:41,930 --> 00:02:44,410
You'd be amazed what they come up with.

44
00:02:44,410 --> 00:02:47,400
FATHER: And of course, that's something they can put on their phone

45
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:50,000
and take home to show all their friends.

46
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,250
SARAH: Exactly. And then they also build a robot in the shape of a human,

47
00:02:55,250 --> 00:03:00,850
and they decorate it and program it so that it can move its arms and legs.

48
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:05,790
FATHER: Perfect. So, is it the same price as the Tiny Engineers?

49
00:03:05,790 --> 00:03:09,910
SARAH: It's just a bit more: £50 for the five weeks.

50
00:03:09,910 --> 00:03:12,180
FATHER: And are the classes on a Monday too?

51
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:20,131
SARAH: They used to be, but we found it didn't give our staff enough time to clear up after the first workshop,

52
00:03:20,131 --> 00:03:22,300
so we moved them to Wednesdays.

53
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:26,060
The classes are held in the morning from ten to eleven.

54
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:28,990
FATHER: OK. That's better for me actually.

55
00:03:28,990 --> 00:03:31,170
And what about the location?

56
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:34,000
Where exactly are the workshops held?

57
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:35,873
SARAH: They're in building 10A -

58
00:03:35,873 --> 00:03:39,520
there's a big sign on the door, you can't miss it

59
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:42,240
and that's in Fradstone Industrial Estate.

60
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:43,100
FATHER: Sorry?

61
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:53,570
SARAH: Fradstone - that's F-R-A-D-S-T-O-N-E.

62
00:03:53,570 --> 00:03:56,710
FATHER: And that's in Grasford, isn't it?

63
00:03:56,710 --> 00:03:58,890
SARAH: Yes, up past the station.

64
00:03:58,890 --> 00:04:01,600
FATHER: And will I have any parking problems there?

65
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:04,300
SARAH: No, there's always plenty available.

66
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:08,000
So would you like to enrol Lydia and Carly now?

67
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:08,870
FATHER: OK.

68
00:04:08,870 --> 00:04:11,000
SARAH: So can I have your full name...
